    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 the only test class (`AcceptanceTest`) plus its helpers make up the whole suite, and it passes with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` rule following the existing `bulkSignupWarning` pattern (a derived, read-time flag rather than a stored column):\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only boolean `capacityWarning` property to the `OwnerDto` schema (the DTO is code-generated from this spec at build time).\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added:\n   - Constants `CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` and `CITY_CAPACITY = 50`.\n   - A `@Mapping(target = \"capacityWarning\", expression = \"java(capacityWarning(owner))\")`.\n   - A `capacityWarning(owner)` method that counts *other* owners in the same city (case-insensitively, matching the existing hard-limit check in `rejectWhenCityAtCapacity`, and excluding the owner itself), returning `true` when that pre-existing count is in `[40, 50)` \u2014 i.e. 40\u201349.\n\nThe window is `>= 40 && < 50`, so the hard rejection at 50 (unchanged in `OwnerRestControllerV1.rejectWhenCityAtCapacity`) stays the boundary: an owner can only be created when the city holds 0\u201349, and it warns for 40\u201349. The test fills a fresh city to 40, creates the 41st owner (40 pre-existing), and reads back `capacityWarning: true`.",
